---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url, evidenceUrl } from "../lib/specs";
import experiments from "../data/experiments.json";
import work from "../data/work.json";
const taskCount = work.items.reduce((n, item) => n + item.total, 0);
---
<Base title="The radio you already own">
  <section class="hero">
    <div><p class="kicker"><span class="live-dot"></span> OPEN HARDWARE RESEARCH / 01 OCT 2026</p><h1>The radio<br />you <em>already own.</em></h1><p class="lede">An ordinary ESP32 has an extraordinary hidden receiver. We’re finding out how useful it is—with an RTL-SDR V4 as a companion, and an FPGA route worth investigating.</p><div class="actions"><a class="primary-link" href={url("work/")}>Explore the experiments ↗</a><a class="text-link" href={url("compare/")}>Compare the two receivers →</a></div></div>
    <div class="instrument">
      <div class="instrument-head"><span>RX / INTERNAL MODEM PATH</span><span class="tag">DESIGN ILLUSTRATION</span></div>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 460 240" role="img" aria-label="Illustrated ESP32 signal path, not measured spectrum">
        <defs><pattern id="grid" width="23" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"><path d="M23 0H0V24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity=".12" /></pattern></defs><rect width="460" height="240" fill="url(#grid)" />
        <g font-family="monospace" font-size="10" fill="currentColor" opacity=".6"><text x="9" y="18">2.4 GHz</text><text x="357" y="18">I/Q BASEBAND</text></g>
        <path d="M20 106q8-62 16 0t16 0t16 0t16 0t16 0" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" fill="none" /><path d="M101 106H147M285 106H337" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 5" />
        <rect x="148" y="56" width="136" height="105" rx="8" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-opacity=".65" /><text x="216" y="94" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace" font-size="15" fill="var(--ink)">ESP32</text><text x="216" y="115" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace" font-size="10" fill="var(--muted)">RADIO · ADC · SRAM</text><text x="216" y="143" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--accent)">UNDOCUMENTED I/Q PATH</text>
        <path d="M339 75H361V107H383V75H405V107H427" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" fill="none" /><path d="M339 139H361V107H383V139H405V107H427" stroke="var(--soc)" stroke-width="2" fill="none" /><text x="436" y="80" font-family="monospace" font-size="10" fill="var(--accent)">I</text><text x="436" y="144" font-family="monospace" font-size="10" fill="var(--soc)">Q</text><g font-family="monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--muted)"><text x="20" y="209">ANTENNA</text><text x="177" y="209">SHORT CAPTURE</text><text x="340" y="209">UART → HOST</text></g>
      </svg>
      <div class="instrument-stats"><div><strong>2.4 GHz</strong><span>Where we start</span></div><div><strong>80 MS/s</strong><span>Upstream nominal maximum</span></div><div><strong>16,380</strong><span>Pairs per snapshot maximum</span></div></div><p class="instrument-caption">Fast acquisition. Small windows. The transfer gap is the experiment.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="fact-strip"><div><small>BOARD / IDENTIFIED</small><strong>ESP32-D0WD-V3</strong><span>Revision 3.1 · original LX6</span></div><div><small>FLASH / MEASURED</small><strong>4 MB · 40 MHz crystal</strong><span>CP2102 USB-to-UART bridge</span></div><div><small>COMPANION / IDENTIFIED</small><strong>RTL-SDR Blog V4</strong><span>R828D · recognized by driver</span></div><div><small>RADIO TRIAL / STATUS</small><strong>Not yet run</strong><span>Firmware still the GPIO test</span></div></div>
  <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / THE QUESTION</p><h2>Wide snapshots. Useful signals?</h2></div><p>The interesting number isn’t just the ADC rate. It’s how much of the real radio activity reaches the host.</p></div><div class="split"><div class="prose-note"><p><a href="https://espargos.net/espsdr/">ESPARGOS</a> exposed an undocumented route from the Wi-Fi modem to raw samples. Its firmware supports the original ESP32, but our unit has not yet been tested as a receiver.</p><p>We’ll establish repeatability, measure gaps, and then ask which applications survive those limits. An FPGA may help process or move data—but the original chip must first expose that data fast enough.</p><a href={url("sources/")}>Read the primary sources and their limits ↗</a></div><div class="note-box"><p class="eyebrow">THE FIRST HARDWARE FINDING</p><h3>Expected AtomVM. Observed GPIO test.</h3><p>A fresh boot identifies <code>hello_world</code> at 160 MHz, toggling the pins. We’ll preserve this image before changing firmware, then evaluate AtomVM separately.</p><a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/board-identification/README.md")}>Open the board record →</a></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / A MEASURABLE PLAN</p><h2>{experiments.length} experiments. {taskCount} explicit tasks.</h2></div><p>Each proposal has a design, acceptance scenarios, tasks and an evidence gate. Hardware acceptance remains open.</p></div><div class="card-grid">{experiments.map((item) => <article class="research-card"><span class="card-number">{String(item.number).padStart(2,"0")}</span><span class="pill">Proposed</span><h3>{item.short}</h3><p>{item.summary}</p><div class="card-bottom"><span>{item.depends.length ? `${item.depends.length} prerequisites` : "Start here"}</span><a href={`${url("work/")}?work=${item.id}`}>Read proposal ↗</a></div></article>)}</div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / CAPTURE BUDGET EXPLORER</p><h2>80 million samples. A fraction of a millisecond.</h2></div><p>A calculation from upstream buffer limits. This is a model, not a measurement of the board or UART.</p></div><div class="calculator"><div class="calc-controls"><label>ADC SAMPLE RATE<select id="sample-rate"><option value="80">80 MS/s</option><option value="40">40 MS/s</option><option value="16">16 MS/s</option></select></label><label>OUTPUT PRECISION<select id="sample-bits"><option value="10">10-bit I + 10-bit Q</option><option value="8">8-bit I + 8-bit Q</option></select></label><label>ASSUMED UART RATE<select id="uart-baud"><option value="921600">921,600 baud</option><option value="460800">460,800 baud</option><option value="115200">115,200 baud</option></select></label></div><div class="calc-results" aria-live="polite"><div><strong id="capture-duration">0.205 ms</strong><span>Radio time in one snapshot</span></div><div><strong id="transfer-duration">444.3 ms</strong><span>Ideal payload transfer, 8N1</span></div><div><strong id="capture-duty">0.046%</strong><span>Ideal capture coverage ceiling</span></div></div><div class="calc-timeline" aria-label="Illustrated acquisition window and transfer gap"><div id="capture-segment" class="capture"></div><div class="gap"></div></div><p class="calc-note">16,380 complex pairs. UART assumes ten wire bits per byte. Coverage = capture ÷ (capture + transfer). Packing, commands, processing and scheduling add time; actual baud compatibility remains untested. Compact FFT output reduces volume, but does not establish gap-free RF capture.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ESPARGOS/esp-sdr/blob/550fadea4d00a9e26ce921c5832167becb3dc20c/main/targets/esp32/receiver.c">Source: original ESP32 capture implementation ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">04 / EVIDENCE BEFORE ASSUMPTIONS</p><h2>Start with what’s actually connected.</h2></div><a class="text-link" href={url("evidence/")}>Browse evidence →</a></div><div class="split"><pre class="terminal">{`$ esptool … flash-id
Chip type: ESP32-D0WD-V3 (revision v3.1)
Features: Dual Core, 240MHz capability
Crystal frequency: 40MHz
Detected flash size: 4MB

Boot: hello_world · 160 MHz
all pins → 1 / all pins → 0`}</pre><div class="prose-note"><p>These lines summarize recorded board logs. The raw output, metadata and limits are one click away. No SDR firmware was written.</p><p>The RTL-SDR probe identifies Blog V4 and R828D. Its tuner-specific test does not establish sustained streaming.</p><p><a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/rtl-identification/README.md")}>RTL-SDR identity ↗</a> · <a href={url("ledger/")}>Accepted observations ↗</a></p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 / FROM DEV BOARD TO RECEIVER</p><h2>A reversible first SDR trial.</h2></div><p>This is the proposed procedure. Firmware installation has not been performed.</p></div><div class="split"><article class="note-box"><h3>Install and capture</h3><ol><li>Confirm the CP2102 serial identity, close other console tools and preserve the entire 4 MB flash locally. Verify its hash and prepare restoration.</li><li>Use the <a href="https://espargos.net/espsdr/app/flash.html">ESPARGOS browser flasher</a>, selecting the original ESP32 target. Its pinned firmware requires at least 2 MB flash; this board has 4 MB.</li><li>Open the <a href="https://espargos.net/espsdr/app/">ESP-SDR web app</a> in a browser supporting Web Serial and connect the UART bridge.</li><li>Start in the normal 2.4 GHz band at conservative settings. Save raw I/Q and FFT snapshots, settings, timestamps and errors. Compare source-on/source-off before attempting decoding.</li></ol><a href={url("work/?work=the-board-can-be-restored-after-an-sdr-trial")}>Preservation and recovery gate →</a></article><article class="note-box"><h3>Choose an application that fits</h3><p>Begin with Wi-Fi-channel occupancy and controlled interference observations, wideband FFT demonstrations or repeatable short-burst capture. Continuous demodulation, reliable packet sniffing, direction finding and calibrated RF measurement each need additional work.</p><p>Standard installation replaces the running application. Keeping AtomVM in charge requires a native worker or port integration; the Erlang VM should control captures and metadata rather than carry the full high-rate sample stream.</p><p>For longer recordings, HF/VHF/UHF reception and mature demodulators, use your existing RTL-SDR V4.</p><a href={url("work/?work=atomvm-can-coordinate-a-native-radio-worker")}>AtomVM integration proposal →</a></article></div></section>
</Base>
<script>
  function calculate() {
    const rate = Number((document.querySelector("#sample-rate") as HTMLSelectElement).value) * 1e6;
    const bits = Number((document.querySelector("#sample-bits") as HTMLSelectElement).value);
    const baud = Number((document.querySelector("#uart-baud") as HTMLSelectElement).value);
    const capture = 16380 / rate;
    const transfer = Math.ceil(16380 * bits * 2 / 8) * 10 / baud;
    const duty = capture / (capture + transfer);
    document.querySelector("#capture-duration")!.textContent = `${(capture * 1000).toFixed(3)} ms`;
    document.querySelector("#transfer-duration")!.textContent = `${(transfer * 1000).toFixed(1)} ms`;
    document.querySelector("#capture-duty")!.textContent = `${(duty * 100).toFixed(3)}%`;
    (document.querySelector("#capture-segment") as HTMLElement).style.width = `${duty * 100}%`;
  }
  document.querySelectorAll(".calc-controls select").forEach((select) => select.addEventListener("change", calculate));
  calculate();
</script>
